When I first implemented the MC3 nerf (and when I started this thread)
I was concerned that I was over doing it, for just the reasons you
gave; but now that I've ascended a few characters with the MC3 nerf
I'm not at all worried about it. The only difference I actually notice
is that vampires and wraiths are a bit more dangerous now. They're
still no where near as dangerous as mind-flayers, but you can't just
completely ignore them anymore.

Regarding the altered bones loading probability, I don't think it
significantly decreases the likelihood of you seeing bones in a game.
The fact is that the numbers of bones that are _created_ is less than
1 per game, and so the average number of bones you will see must be
less than 1 per game regardless of what the probability per level is.
Whether the probability is 1/3 per level or 1/5 per level, the pool of
bones files is always going to be low. The probability is not the
bottle-neck; so it won't greatly affect the average number of bones
that you see.

In other news, I've ascended another character with my latest
resistance changes. I was a bit surprised by how low the damage was
with the resistance nerf. I was expecting it to be a lot harder. I've
just been looking through the code to try to see what's going on. What
I've noticed is that having very good AC actually significantly
reduces the damage you receive, as well as reducing the probability
that you'll actually get hit. In my opinion the damage is getting
reduced a bit too strongly, and I may end up changing it, but there's
another issue that needs to be changed first:   this damage reduction
from AC actually applies not just to weapons and bites etc, but also
to elemental attacks. Furthermore, half_physical_damage also applies
to elemental attacks. In my opinion this is a bug. So I'm going to
move that damage reduction so that it does not apply to cold/fire/
shock. I'll give that a try, and maybe after that I'll think about
changing the to-hit formula. I'd like to make physical attackers, such
as minotaurs, be no more dangerous in the early-mid game, but not a
complete pushover in the late game. As it stands, there are just a
handful of monsters that one has to pay attention to in the late game.
Mind flayers, sea monsters, disenchanters, liches, green slime,
cockatrices, ... and that's about all. I want to expand the list a
bit.

Also, I've made it so that eating corpses which "taste terrible"
abuses your con. attribute. My aim with this is to encourage players
to live off their real food rather than just eating everything they
kill. It's my opinion that it would be pretty unhealthy to just going
around eating all sorts of raw meat off the ground. As it turns out,
this change doesn't make a lot of difference anyway because con is
excised just by not being hungry or satiated; so you still end up
gaining con even if you go around eating strange stuff.

